title: Orphan scan
tags: [[Orphans]]
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: The scan reports a file missing only when a readable ancestor proves it absent and no offline mount point is above it; the endpoints, and the mass-orphan circuit breaker.

The scan is driven by the ''tracked set'', not the eligible tree: every metarecord that
still resolves an `mfr_path` is checked against the disk, so a subtree deleted while
unwatched is still found. A record is reported only when ''a readable ancestor proves the
file absent'': the scan climbs from the path to its nearest existing ancestor, and
reports an orphan only if that ancestor could be listed and the path is absent below it.
An unreadable (`EACCES`) or stale-handle (`ENOTCONN`) ancestor, or a broken symlink
still present at the path, yields ''unknown'', never an orphan.

That guard alone would not save a removable volume — an unmounted mount point reads back
as an empty, readable directory — so paths at or below an offline mount point are
"unknown" before the walk is attempted ([[Offline subtrees]]). Together the two guards
are what make acting on the answer safe: neither a permission drop nor an unplugged
drive can orphan a subtree.

|!Route |!Does |
|<<cmd "POST /repos/:repo/orphans/scan">> |read-only: `{"count": N, "orphans": [{"uuid", "stale_path"}]}` |
|<<cmd "POST /repos/:repo/orphans/mark">> |writes `orphan = true` on both kinds and unmarks the rest, one revision: `{"orphans": N, "marked": A, "unmarked": B}` |
|<<cmd "POST /repos/:repo/orphans/clear">> |`{"uuids": [...]}`: for each still missing (re-verified), `mfr_path_old` captured, `mfr_path = nothing`, cascade — one revision: `{"cleared": N}` |
|<<cmd "POST /repos/:repo/orphans/relink">> |a task (`202`), result `relinked`, `conflicts`, `skipped` ([[Relinking orphans]]) |

Marking counts both populations — the missing (which needs the disk) and the deleted
(`mfr_path IS ABSENT`) — with the scan's guards unchanged. Deleting the marked set is not
a component of its own: it is `POST /query/delete` over `orphan = true`.

!! The mass-orphan circuit breaker

Independently of mount points: one event batch that would orphan a very large number of
metarecords is a symptom, not a deletion — a filesystem going away, an unmount the
daemon did not recognise, a mount point moved under it. The executor refuses a cascade
of more than `orphan-cascade-limit` metarecords (default 10 000, `0` disables it,
[[Daemon configuration]]): the cascade is skipped, a warning logged, the metadata left
as it was. Recoverable, not fatal: the paths just stay stale, and a genuinely huge
deletion is confirmed with `mf orphan clear`, which re-verifies every path first.

(The pairing query of the SQL era had to seek, not scan, or it was quadratic on a build
tree full of deleted artifacts; the store's keyed reads made that constraint moot.)
